<!--

    Transitive closure with eventless transitions.

    A chart can compute, not only react. The <datamodel> seeds the Prolog
    facts p(a,b), p(b,c), p(c,d). The single working state has an
    eventless transition whose guard looks for a missing link (p(X,Y) and
    p(Y,Z) but not yet p(X,Z)) and whose body asserts it. Needing no
    event, the chart keeps taking that transition, adding one new edge
    each time, until the guard can no longer be satisfied. A second
    guarded transition then collects the finished relation and moves to
    the final state. It demonstrates eventless (automatic) transitions,
    guards that run real Prolog queries, and the chart's own logical
    database used as working memory.

    Run the chart from the Examples menu.
    Keep the Logger open and SXML trace selected.
    
-->

<statechart version="0.2" initial="s">
    <datamodel>
        p(a,b). 
        p(b,c). 
        p(c,d).
    </datamodel>
    <state id="s">
        <go if="p(X,Y), p(Y,Z), \+p(X,Z)">
            assert(p(X,Z))
        </go>
        <go to="f" if="findall(p(X,Y), p(X,Y), List)">
            format("Closure: ~w", [List])
        </go>
    </state>
    <final id="f"/>
</statechart>


